<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglesey</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ynys Môn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anglesey, in Welsh Ynys Môn and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Mona, is the largest island off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, separated from the mainland by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menai-strait">Menai Strait</a>. A centre of the druids, it was attacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 60 CE and conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a> in 77, and its fertile farmland later made it the granary and royal heartland of the kingdom of Gwynedd. Edward I built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaumaris-castle">Beaumaris Castle</a> there after his conquest of Wales.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglesey.jpg" alt="Anglesey" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Buck · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_the_Anglesey_Coastal_Path_(geograph_6222502).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
